  "summary": "A retired San Diego County Sheriff's commander writes an op-ed urging a countywide ALPR standard after revelations that La Mesa's 20 Flock cameras were searched 3.13 million times in seven months, mostly by outside agencies, with reasons redacted. He cites past misuse including Sheriff's Office searches on behalf of federal immigration agents and calls for audit transparency, purpose limitation, and withdrawal from the statewide network.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "those 20 cameras were searched more than 3.13 million times in seven months, more than 14,000 searches a day",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "La Mesa produced them with the reason for every single search blacked out",
      "paragraph_idx": 7
    },
    {
      "quote": "the Sheriff's Office had run searches on behalf of Customs and Border Protection or the Border Patrol, even though state law prohibits sharing this data with federal agencies",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "Even the San Diego Police Department logged nearly 4,000 searches simply as \"other\" before ending that option",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    },
    {
      "quote": "Every search should be tied to a real case or report number, with no catch-alls like \"other\" or \"investigation.\"",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    }
  ],
